# CWL (Common Workflow Language)

## Introduction

CWL is a specification for describing workflows i.e. task A -> task B -> task C.

=> https://www.commonwl.org/ More details on CWL

To set up in guix:

>> guix install cwltool ccwl

By default, `cwltool` takes in yaml files e.g.

```
# filename: hello_world.cwl
cwlVersion: v1.2

class: CommandLineTool
baseCommand: echo

inputs:
  message:
    type: string
    default: "Hello world"
    inputBinding:
      position: 1

outputs: []
```

And running `cwltool hello_world.cwl` gives the result.

`CCWL (Concise Common Workflow Language)` is a Scheme DSL for CWL that is simpler and more consise.

The above example will look like:

```
(command #:inputs (message #:type string)
		 #:run "echo" message
		 #:outputs (printed-message #:type stdout))
```

And run it using:

>> ccwl compile hello.scm > hello.cwl
>> cwltool hello.cwl

=> https://ccwl.systemreboot.net/manual/dev/en/ More complex examples for ccwl

## Potential Usage

CWL, Ravanan and Slurm will be the backbone for our asynchronous jobs.

We'll have to build tooling or add features that can complement these like:

- Timeouts for workflows
- Progress report support
- Retry mechanisms

=> https://git.systemreboot.net/ravanan/tree/README.md Ravanan
=> https://slurm.schedmd.com/overview.html Slurm
